# When studying the taxicab distance:
# d(W(i,j)+1, W(i,j+1)+1)

# For j ≥ 3, the distance equals an earlier column of the Wythoff array:
# d(W(i,j)+1, W(i,j+1)+1) = W(i, j-3)

# j = 3 → gives floor(i * phi)
# j = 4 → gives column 1 of the Wythoff array
# j = 5 → gives column 2
# j = 6 → gives column 3
# ...

# So the shifted distance effectively moves 3 columns to the left.

# Because each row follows a Fibonacci recurrence,
# the distance sequences inherit this structure across columns.

